---
permalink: /feed.xml
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
{#-
  RSS 2.0 for the Insights articles.

  Two reasons this exists, and the second is the one that pays for it.

  A news section without a feed is a news section nobody can subscribe to. That
  is the ordinary reason and it has been true since the section was built.

  The other is that a feed is the join between writing an article once and it
  appearing everywhere else. Buffer, Zapier, Make and every other scheduler
  takes an RSS URL as a trigger: point one of them here and a new article turns
  into a LinkedIn post on its own, with the headline, the summary and a link
  back. Nobody writes anything twice, and the article stays the canonical
  version on a domain we own rather than a post inside somebody else's product.

  RSS 2.0 rather than Atom because the schedulers all read it and several of
  them still do not read Atom properly.

  Absolute URLs throughout. A feed is read somewhere else by definition, so a
  relative link in it is a link to nothing.
-#}
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">
  <channel>
    <title>{{ site.name }} Insights</title>
    <link>{{ site.url }}/insights/</link>
    <description>{{ site.tagline }}</description>
    <language>en</language>
    <atom:link href="{{ site.url }}/feed.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/>
    {%- set sorted = collections.insights | reverse -%}
    {%- if sorted | length %}
    <lastBuildDate>{{ sorted[0].date | rssDate }}</lastBuildDate>
    {%- endif %}
    {%- for post in sorted %}
    <item>
      <title>{{ post.data.title }}</title>
      <link>{{ site.url }}{{ post.url }}</link>
      {#- The guid is what a scheduler uses to decide whether it has seen an
          item before. It has to be stable: if it changes, the same article is
          posted to LinkedIn a second time. The URL contains the publication
          date and the slug, neither of which changes after publishing. -#}
      <guid isPermaLink="true">{{ site.url }}{{ post.url }}</guid>
      <pubDate>{{ post.date | rssDate }}</pubDate>
      {%- if post.data.author %}
      <dc:creator>{{ post.data.author }}</dc:creator>
      {%- endif %}
      <description>{{ post.data.summary }}</description>
      {%- for tag in post.data.tags %}
      <category>{{ tag }}</category>
      {%- endfor %}
      {#- Several schedulers will attach the enclosure image to the social post
          rather than relying on the link preview, which is the difference
          between a post with a picture and a post with a grey box. -#}
      {#- Not every image field holds a site-relative path. Two of the older
          articles still point at pbs.twimg.com, and prefixing site.url to
          those produced a URL with two schemes in it, which every reader
          would have silently dropped. Absolute stays absolute. -#}
      {%- if post.data.image %}
      {%- set imgSrc = post.data.image if "://" in post.data.image else site.url + post.data.image %}
      <enclosure url="{{ imgSrc }}" type="image/{{ post.data.image | imageType }}" length="0"/>
      {%- endif %}
    </item>
    {%- endfor %}
  </channel>
</rss>
